=== THE SOFTANZA SOUND SHOWCASE ===
    19 examples. Each says what to listen FOR.

  01. naive-saw           GRITTY. Harmonics folding back as tones nobody played.
           (the bug plate 1 found)
  02. band-limited-saw    The SAME sweep, clean. This is the PolyBLEP fix.
           (post-SN6)
  03. four-timbres        One pitch, four waveforms: sine, triangle, square, saw.
           (SN2 oscillators)
  04. beats               440 and 443 Hz. Three pulses a second -- the difference.
           (gallery plate 4)
  05. click-vs-fade       Cut dead at 1 s, faded at 2.5 s. Only one clicks.
           (SN3's ramp, gallery plate 2)
  06. filter-sweep        A saw through a lowpass opening up. Q is high, so it whistles.
           (SN2 filters)
  07. resonance           The same note at Q 0.7 then Q 8. Listen for the hump.
           (gallery plate 5)
  08. envelope-shapes     Pluck, pad, stab -- one oscillator, three envelopes.
           (SN2 ADSR)
  09. echo                A blip, then the same blip down a corridor.
           (SN2 delay)
  10. stereo-pan          A tone walking from left to right. Use headphones.
           (SN2 pan)
  11. chord-and-arpeggio  A major chord, then its notes one at a time.
           (SN2 mix)
  12. bell                A struck bell: fast attack, long decay, a little room.
           (SN2 composition)
  13. earcons-five        The semantic values: danger, warning, info, success -- and muted, which is SILENT.
           (SS1, Rule 118)
  14. earcon-priority     Eight rapid repeats heard twice, then a danger alert that silences four successes.
           (SS's priority contract)
  15. voice-plain         A spoken sentence, straight from the platform.
           (VC1/VC2)
  16. voice-prosody       The same words at normal speed, then 40% slower, via SSML.
           (VC1 prosody)
  17. voice-as-sound      A voice through a telephone, then down a corridor. THE point: a voice is a sound.
           (VC1 architecture)
  18. voice-and-earcon    An alert, then the phrase that says WHICH -- the semantic bridge, previewed.
           (VC4 preview)
  19. the-piece           A short composition using most of the above at once.
           (everything)

